    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today with a clear emphasis on top-of-the-order speed and platoon advantages. **James Wood** (70.34) and **Corbin Carroll** (69.70) anchor our production, benefitting significantly from aggressive base-stealing opportunities against slow deliveries from opposing hurlers. **Wood** is our primary engine today; his leadoff spot and favorable matchup against Alcantara provide the highest efficiency ceiling on the roster. Meanwhile, **Trevor Story** (71.87) offers the highest score of the day, with park factors and favorable wind conditions augmenting a strong projection against Martinez. We are also leaning heavily into **Vinnie Pasquantino** (64.21), whose platoon edge against Hanifee provides a massive 14% lift, making him an essential start despite a moderate opposing xERA.\n\nThe benching process remains disciplined, focused on maximizing our daily efficiency cap. **Cole Young** (42.70) was the closest cut, failing to crack the lineup due to a bottom-third batting order position that limits his run-scoring probability compared to the high-upside spots held by **Josh Naylor** (68.36) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.56). We have chosen to keep **Jose Caballero** (39.32) on the bench entirely, as the matchup against Henderson falls below our 40-point \"Zebras Floor,\" prioritizing efficiency over his stolen base upside in a difficult environment.\n\nStrategic caution is warranted regarding **Seiya Suzuki** (44.29). While his talent is undeniable, he faces a formidable test against deGrom, whose elite xERA (3.18) and high strikeout potential have suppressed **Suzuki**'s score significantly. We are also monitoring **Brice Turang** (54.75), as he draws a tough left-on-left matchup against Rod\u00f3n. However, **Turang** maintains a starting role thanks to his premium #2 slot, which offers enough plate appearance volume to justify the risk. Overall, our lineup is positioned to maximize stolen base potential while mitigating the threat of elite opposing arms like deGrom.",
